    counselling: "Adrenal insufficiency means your adrenal glands do not produce enough stress hormones. You need lifelong replacement with hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone. You must never miss a dose. During illness (fever, vomiting, diarrhoea), you need to DOUBLE or TRIPLE your dose (sick-day rules). Always wear a MedicAlert bracelet. If you cannot keep tablets down due to vomiting, go to hospital for IV hydrocortisone.",
    followUp: "Endocrine clinic in 4-6 weeks after discharge for adrenal antibody testing and bone density baseline. Provide MedicAlert bracelet and sick-day rules card. Educate patient and family on emergency injection of hydrocortisone (provide IM hydrocortisone kit). Annual: electrolytes, renin, DXA.",
    pearls: "Primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease) in Kenya is frequently post-TB (TB destroys adrenal glands) or autoimmune. Hyponatraemia + hyperkalaemia + hypoglycaemia + hypotension is a classic combination pointing to mineralocorticoid deficiency. NEVER give steroids without checking for TB unless you have ruled it out (steroids can reactivate latent TB). Sick-day rules: double or triple hydrocortisone dose during febrile illness, injury, or surgery. If vomiting, give IM/IV hydrocortisone and go to hospital.",
    references: ["ESE Guidelines: Adrenal Insufficiency", "Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Adrenal Disorders", "WHO: Management of Adrenal Crisis"],
